abstract: We study when a reduced description preserves the response to an applied source. For
  systems defined by continuously differentiable state updates and observations a bounded surjective
  linear coarse map gives differentiable descent when the projected maps are constant on each
  fibre. Their common source response is then preserved on the invertible domain. For approximate
  descent an exact error identity separates defects in the update and source coupling from defects
  in the readout and direct term. Resolvent and contour estimates carry these errors and the
  displacement of the reference branch to visible poles and their residues at shifted critical
  roots. In a reciprocal Hilbert-space realisation an isolated finite-dimensional critical subspace
  with a positive crossing matrix has a positive-semidefinite finite-rank residue when the endpoint
  operator is self-adjoint and the complementary inverse is bounded. Source overlap determines
  its visible directions. Arithmetic and reflecting-heat examples separate stationary response
  preservation from memoryless dynamical closure. In the two-mode example pole displacement
  decays geometrically with the retained memory length. The residue error includes an additional
  factor of that length. For a matched vacuum covariance the residue is the zero-mass atom of
  its positive spectral measure. This identification uses the same physical projection and source
  pairing together with the continuation and subtraction conventions and endpoint matching.
